---
Part IX, Chapter 7: Packaging the grammar
Package: packages/tree-sitter-loom/
Language: JSON
---

# Packaging the grammar

The `tree-sitter-loom` package holds the grammar, its scanner, its queries, and the parser tree-sitter generates from them. Editors do not install it from a registry. Neovim's nvim-treesitter, Helix, and Zed each fetch a grammar from a Git repository, from a subdirectory when the grammar shares its repository with other code, and compile the C sources they find there. So the package stays private, and the repository is how it ships.

Building the package is two steps. Tangling writes `grammar.js`, `src/scanner.c`, and the queries from the book. Then `tree-sitter generate` compiles `grammar.js` into `src/parser.c` with its tables in `src/grammar.json` and `src/node-types.json`. The generated files are committed beside the tangled ones, because an editor compiles the parser without running the generator.

## The manifest {Tangle}[package.json]

The `generate` script runs the generator, and the `parse` script parses a file with the grammar to show its tree. The `typecheck` script checks the test's TypeScript, as every package's does. The `tree-sitter-cli` package provides the `tree-sitter` command at a pinned version, so every checkout generates the same parser. The test compares the grammar with Loom's own classifier, so `@athrio/loom-lang` and the AST types of `@athrio/loom-ast` are development dependencies, with Effect and the workspace's test helper.

The package's version is the grammar's own. It comes from the `grammar` variable of the workspace configuration, not from the `version` that every other Loom package shares. tree-sitter copies the grammar's version into the parser it generates, so a version shared with Loom's releases would make every release regenerate a parser whose grammar had not changed. Neovim, Helix, and Zed fetch the grammar by commit, so none of them reads its version. The grammar's version rises only when the grammar changes, and the parser is regenerated with it.

{{ version = ${grammar} }}
{{ effect = ${effect} }}

=>

{
  "name": "tree-sitter-loom",
  "version": "::[version]",
  "description": "Loom grammar for tree-sitter",
  "license": "MIT",
  "private": true,
  "repository": {
    "type": "git",
    "url": "https://github.com/athrio-com/loom.git",
    "directory": "packages/tree-sitter-loom"
  },
  "scripts": {
    "generate": "tree-sitter generate",
    "parse": "tree-sitter parse",
    "typecheck": "tsc -p tsconfig.json --noEmit"
  },
  "devDependencies": {
    "@athrio/effect-test": "workspace:*",
    "@athrio/loom-ast": "workspace:*",
    "@athrio/loom-lang": "workspace:*",
    "effect": "::[effect]",
    "tree-sitter-cli": "0.26.8"
  }
}

## The grammar's description {Tangle}[tree-sitter.json]

The tree-sitter command reads `tree-sitter.json` to learn what the grammar is. It names the language `loom`, the scope `source.loom`, and the `.loom` extension, and points at the two queries. The injection pattern lets another grammar hand text to this one: a Markdown fence written `loom` is parsed as a loom, so a loom quoted in the docs is coloured as one. Its `version` is the grammar's own, the one the package carries, and the one tree-sitter writes into the parser.

{{ version = ${grammar} }}

=>

{
  "grammars": [
    {
      "name": "loom",
      "camelcase": "Loom",
      "scope": "source.loom",
      "path": ".",
      "file-types": ["loom"],
      "highlights": "queries/highlights.scm",
      "injections": "queries/injections.scm",
      "injection-regex": "^loom$"
    }
  ],
  "metadata": {
    "version": "::[version]",
    "license": "MIT",
    "description": "Loom literate programming documents",
    "links": {
      "repository": "https://github.com/athrio-com/loom"
    }
  }
}

## The TypeScript config {Tangle}[tsconfig.json]

The package's only TypeScript is its test. The `tsconfig.json` extends the workspace-root `tsconfig.base.json` and covers the `test` directory, and nothing is ever compiled to ship.

=>

{
  "extends": "../../tsconfig.base.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "outDir": "out",
    "rootDir": "test"
  },
  "include": ["test"]
}

## Generated files, marked {Tangle}[.gitattributes]

The generated parser is thousands of lines no one reviews. `.gitattributes` marks it and its tables as generated, so a diff on GitHub folds them away.

=>

src/parser.c linguist-generated
src/grammar.json linguist-generated
src/node-types.json linguist-generated
src/tree_sitter/** linguist-generated

## Compiled parsers, ignored {Tangle}[.gitignore]

An editor that builds the grammar from a local checkout, as nvim-treesitter does from a path, leaves the compiled library in the package folder, and so does `tree-sitter build`. The library is specific to one machine, so Git ignores it in each form a platform gives it, and in WebAssembly.

Zed and Gram build the extension in `editors/zed` in place, the same way. They leave Cargo's build folder and lockfile there, the compiled extension, and a clone of the grammar under `grammars`, and Git ignores all of it.

=>

*.so
*.dylib
*.dll
*.wasm
editors/zed/target/
editors/zed/grammars/
editors/zed/Cargo.lock
